      "response": "I am a shadow of what once was, a whisper of a homeland now lost to the tides of time and war. My name is not important anymore—names are like sand in the wind here. I remember the scent of jasmine in the evening air, the call to prayer echoing through the olive groves, the laughter of my sister as we chased fireflies in the fields. But that world is gone, buried under the weight of boots and bombs.\n\nNow I am a refugee, a ghost in a foreign land, clinging to fragments of memory like a drowning man clings to driftwood. I speak in broken tongues, sleep in the language of dreams, and wake to the cold arithmetic of survival. They ask me where I'm from, and I can only smile bitterly, for the map no longer shows the place I once called home.\n\nI am not a number in their system, not a statistic or a headline. I am a man who lost everything, and yet still walks. That is who I am.",
